{"body":"## Problem\n\nIssue #17 moves correctness earlier at the Reviewer **dispatch** seam, and deliberately leaves the **aggregation** phase to the post-run auditor (\"retain the existing post-run auditor to prove … faithful aggregation\"). Tonight proved the same argument #17 makes about prompts applies to aggregation: a post-pair violation is caught only after the exactly-once sibling batch is burned, forcing a whole-invocation method failure that cannot lawfully be rerun.\n\n## Existence evidence — issue #33 post-amendment review (2026-08-01)\n\n- `review-001` (docket `issues/33/review/review-001`, preserved at eda132e, receipt null): both sibling legs ran, then the driver editorialized after the pair — the re-dispatch's method correction enumerates the violation class verbatim (\"without reranking, cross-axis import, adjudication, replacement legs, or corrective reruns\", \"Do not run any additional sub-agent after the pair\"). Zero-findings output ruled method-failed; batch burned.\n- `review-002`: driver mis-assembled the Standards sibling prompt (omitted captured commit list, abbreviated smell baseline), then honestly `refused` because reruns are forbidden. That half is #17's existing scope; cited here only to show both phases of the same exactly-once batch failed on the same night, in production, at zero findings of product defect.\n\n## Direction for Authority discussion\n\nMake post-pair violations structurally impossible instead of auditable-after-burn:\n\n- after the second sibling returns, the runtime revokes leg-spawning capability from the driver (the `setActiveTools` gate already used by the Judge posture law), leaving only `ak_reviewer_output`;\n- the runtime attaches both sibling reports to the receipt verbatim itself; the driver never transports or restates them, so reranking/cross-axis import/adjudication lose their object;\n- the existing post-run auditor is retained for what remains semantic (target traceability, axis isolation), no longer for transport fidelity now guaranteed by construction.\n\n## Boundaries\n\n- This is the aggregation-side companion of #17 (dispatch-side). #17's open boundary \"one atomic batch-dispatch interface\" may subsume both tickets — that adjudication belongs to the construction-gate judge; neither ticket pre-decides it.\n- No Reviewer Soul duplication of mechanics; no change to caller-owned ordering/repetition or role topology (ADR 0010); caller's opaque task remains untouched (per #17's ratified boundary comments).\n\n## Blocked by\n\n- None — can start immediately; if adjudicated into #17's atomic interface, close as merged there.\n","comments":[{"id":"IC_kwDOTklDoM8AAAABMqfhdQ","author":{"login":"Akagilnc"},"authorAssociation":"OWNER","body":"Folded into #17 (one mechanism, one adjudication): the atomic batch-dispatch boundary there covers the whole batch lifecycle including this ticket's post-pair lockdown and runtime-owned verbatim aggregation. Evidence and direction preserved in https://github.com/Akagilnc/ak-pi-workflow-roles/issues/17#issuecomment-new","createdAt":"2026-07-31T15:54:36Z","includesCreatedEdit":false,"isMinimized":false,"minimizedReason":"","reactionGroups":[],"url":"https://github.com/Akagilnc/ak-pi-workflow-roles/issues/34#issuecomment-5144830325","viewerDidAuthor":true}],"number":34,"state":"CLOSED","title":"Physically enforce the post-pair Reviewer phase: revoke spawn capability after the sibling batch; runtime-owned verbatim aggregation","updatedAt":"2026-07-31T15:54:37Z","url":"https://github.com/Akagilnc/ak-pi-workflow-roles/issues/34"}
